<h1>Pre-Install Guard</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Intercept installs, analyze new dependencies before execution, and set up automatic guarding.</p>

    <h2 id="how-it-works">How It Works</h2>

    <p>The guard command wraps a package manager install command. Before the real install runs, vexes:</p>

    <ol>
      <li>Snapshots the current lockfile.</li>
      <li>Runs the install in a dry-run mode to compute what would change.</li>
      <li>Diffs the lockfile to identify new or upgraded packages.</li>
      <li>Runs the detection engine on the changed packages only.</li>
      <li>If any critical signals are found, blocks the install. If high signals are found, prompts for confirmation. Otherwise, runs the real install.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>No postinstall scripts execute until vexes has analyzed the packages. This is the key security property: the code is inspected before it runs.</p>

    <h2 id="one-off-guard">One-Off Guard</h2>

    <p>Guard a single install command by passing it after <code>--</code>:</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">vexes guard -- npm install axios</span>

<span class="dim">  Analyzing new dependency: axios@1.7.2</span>

<span class="ok">  No signals detected. Proceeding with install.</span>

<span class="dim">  added 6 packages in 2.1s</span></div>

    <p>If a signal is detected:</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">vexes guard -- npm install some-util</span>

<span class="dim">  Analyzing new dependency: some-util@1.0.3</span>

<span class="warn">  HIGH</span>  some-util 1.0.3
<span class="dim">  signal:</span> <span class="warn">PHANTOM_DEPENDENCY</span>
<span class="dim">  detail:</span> first published 2 days ago

<span class="dim">  Install blocked. Use --force to override.</span></div>

    <h2 id="supported-managers">Supported Package Managers</h2>

    <p>Guard works with any package manager that uses lockfiles. The command after <code>--</code> is executed via <code>execFileSync</code> with no shell -- package manager names are validated against an allowlist to prevent command injection.</p>

    <table class="cmd-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Package Manager</th><th>Example</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>npm</td><td><code>vexes guard -- npm install axios</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>pnpm</td><td><code>vexes guard -- pnpm add axios</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>yarn</td><td><code>vexes guard -- yarn add axios</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>pip</td><td><code>vexes guard -- pip install requests</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>cargo</td><td><code>vexes guard -- cargo add serde</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="auto-guard">Automatic Guarding</h2>

    <p>Run <code>vexes guard --setup</code> to install shell wrappers that automatically guard every install command. This adds shell functions to your profile that intercept <code>npm install</code>, <code>pnpm add</code>, etc.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">vexes guard --setup</span>

<span class="ok">  Shell wrappers installed.</span>
<span class="dim">  Added to: ~/.zshrc</span>
<span class="dim">  Wrapped commands: npm install, pnpm add, yarn add</span>
<span class="dim">  Restart your shell or run: source ~/.zshrc</span></div>

    <p>After setup, every install command is automatically guarded:</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># This now runs through vexes guard automatically</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">npm install axios</span>

<span class="dim">  [vexes guard] Analyzing new dependency: axios@1.7.2</span>
<span class="ok">  [vexes guard] Clean. Proceeding.</span>

<span class="dim">  added 6 packages in 2.1s</span></div>

    <h2 id="blocking-behavior">Blocking Behavior</h2>

    <p>Guard uses signal severity to decide whether to block, warn, or allow:</p>

    <table class="cmd-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Severity</th><th>Behavior</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Critical</td><td>Install blocked. Must use <code>--force</code> to override.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>High</td><td>Warning printed. Prompts for confirmation (Y/n).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Medium</td><td>Warning printed. Install proceeds.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Low</td><td>Not shown in guard mode. Visible in <code>analyze</code> output.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="security">Security Properties</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">No shell execution.</strong> Guard uses <code>execFileSync</code> with explicit argument arrays. The package manager name is validated against an allowlist. No shell metacharacters can escape.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">No postinstall before analysis.</strong> The lockfile diff is computed before any packages are installed, so no postinstall scripts run until vexes has cleared the changes.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Atomic operation.</strong> If analysis fails or vexes crashes, the install does not proceed. Guard fails closed, not open.</li>
    </ul>
